Yes. I think you’re describing exposure, not merely competitiveness.

To compete openly is to reveal information: I want this. I think I might be capable of this. This matters to me. Once you’ve said that, other people can evaluate you against the claim. Privacy protects you from some of that evaluation, but it also prevents other people—and sometimes yourself—from seeing the complete signal.

Wanting to be sure of yourself before becoming visible makes sense. The catch is that certainty usually arrives partly through exposure to reality. If you require certainty before exposure, you can create a loop:

\text{Privacy}\rightarrow\text{less testing}\rightarrow
\text{less evidence}\rightarrow\text{less certainty}\rightarrow
\text{more privacy}

So I wouldn’t recommend becoming radically more public. I’d separate testing from broadcasting. You can enter arenas, make commitments, keep score, take risks, and discover your limits without turning your identity into a public performance.

And your point about work is important. In many cultures, the question “What do you do?” is practically a socially acceptable substitute for “Who are you?” A job starts as an economic role, then accumulates status, tribe, routines, competence, relationships, vocabulary, expectations, and a story about oneself. Eventually the mind predicts the world partly through that role.

Then changing or losing the role isn’t merely:

\text{old job}\rightarrow\text{new job}

It’s closer to:

\text{old self-model}\rightarrow\boxed{?}\rightarrow\text{new self-model}

That middle region can feel strange because homeostasis isn’t only bodily. We maintain relatively stable models of who I am, what people expect from me, what I’m good at, where I rank, what tomorrow looks like. A major role supplies enormous amounts of that structure.

That’s where your desire to be “in sync” gets interesting.

I wouldn’t try to find another identity strong enough to replace the old one. That’s just swapping uniforms. I’d build synchronization between three layers:

Private self ↔ enacted self ↔ public self

The private self contains far more than anyone needs access to. The enacted self is what you actually choose, practice, build and compete at. The public self is the deliberately limited portion you’re willing to let other people know.

Those three don’t have to be identical. They just shouldn’t contradict each other.

So privacy doesn’t require hiding. Privacy means controlling resolution.

And confidence doesn’t require announcing “I am him.” A much more stable confidence eventually becomes:

I know what I am because I’ve repeatedly watched what I do.

Then being discovered isn’t quite as threatening, because someone seeing you accurately doesn’t create the identity or take it away. They’re simply observing something you’ve already tested privately.

That may be the synchronization you’re reaching for: not maximum visibility, but minimum internal contradiction.